      "id": "PRR_kwDOSiSx0c8AAAABSnMwog",
      "state": "COMMENTED",
      "body": "## Decision\n\n- Needs reviewer attention before upstream: findings remain.\n\n## Confirmed findings\n\n- Test matrix excludes a JavaScript preview scenario under RIPR-SPEC-0082 partial_scope; spec wording covers JS but no positive test asserts JS file_count/sample_paths behavior across budgets.\n\n## Verification questions\n\n- Confirm human-renderer substring matchers for '<N> Python files analyzed under preview support' / 'Python file analyzed under preview support' are duplicated as two literals per enabled case; budget=1 at enabled=true hits the singular arm, but a singular literal with no enabled case is asserted via separate `display file analyzed` block — easy to rot if wording changes.\n- Confirm rIPR-SPEC-0082 and .ripr/traceability.toml map the new controls; tests are anchored under spec ID. However, the spec's wording says 'enabled Python and TypeScript/JavaScript advisories' but the test harness excludes a JS preview scenario even though `javascript_awaited_roots` exists — no positive coverage of JS preview path under partial_scope.\n\n## Refuted\n\n- budget 2 'changed = 0' assertion fails because the source filter is too aggressive and removes the lib.rs subject.; refuted because: Assertion checks `findings.iter().any(... src/lib.rs)` survives all five scenarios and asserts preview_findings==1 only when expected_preview==1 (budget 2). With budget=2 scope.selects lib.rs and one preview file, source filter only strips the second preview file; lib.rs remains.\n- Partial_scope is constructed elsewhere without language gating; passing it into the new filter risks false-positive filters for non-preview paths (e.g. Rust files) and silently shrinks advisory selection.; refuted because: Filter requires `route(...).is_some_and(\\|l\\| l != Perl && languages.contains(&l))`. Rust paths route to LanguageId::Rust which is enabled by default, but Rust preview adapter is not enabled in the test (no rust language route = not in languages list for preview). For Rust the branch could fire; the original filter already dropped absent/absent_changed, and adding `!scope.selects` only tightens. Bar this audit supports the claim that route() values for enabled preview languages produce narrower outputs without affecting Rust findings: changed_files_by_language for 'python'/'typescript' is what the test asserts, and Rust subject survives by findings presence.\n\n## Parked follow-ups\n\n- Pre-repair red (Cargo101) and shaping failure (wrong literal file order at budget 2) are excluded; final proof uses corrected Rust-first literal ordering without changing product ordering. Concern: shaping failure suggests brittleness but is excluded from proof.\n\n## Evidence gaps\n\n- Model proof intent was rejected by deterministic validation.\n",
